 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per voxel, but Sobel requires more texture fetches (up to 18 neighbors) than Central Difference (6 neighbors).
-- **Memory:** Minimal overhead; requires only the volume texture and a mode flag.
-- **Strengths:** Flexible; allows runtime tuning for noise vs. performance. Central difference is fast and isotropic. Sobel reduces noise artifacts.
-- **Limitations:** Sobel is computationally heavier. Boundary handling complexity increases with Sobel.
-- **When to use:** Use Central Difference for smooth, low-noise data or performance-critical applications. Use Sobel for noisy data where edge preservation is critical [S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per voxel, as it involves a fixed number of memory accesses and arithmetic operations.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory.
+- **Strengths:** Central differences provide high accuracy for smooth fields. Sobel estimation offers better noise robustness. The selection mechanism allows runtime adaptation.
+- **Limitations:** Central differences are sensitive to noise. Sobel kernels are more expensive than central differences due to larger stencils.
+- **When to use:** Use Central Differences for clean, high-resolution data. Use Sobel for noisy data or when smoother normals are desired [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Gradient selection is a common pattern in GPU-based volume rendering shaders, particularly in WebGL and OpenGL implementations where hardware acceleration is leveraged [S1, unofficial]. It emerged as a practical solution to the trade-off between gradient accuracy and noise sensitivity in real-time rendering [S2, unofficial].
+Gradient estimation via finite differences is a fundamental technique in volume rendering for computing surface normals from scalar fields [S2, unofficial]. The specific implementation of selecting between Central and Sobel modes is documented in community resources [S1, unofficial]. The theoretical basis for finite difference gradients is covered in standard volume rendering literature [S2, unofficial].
 
